\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Definition}
\title{Weighted graph differentials and adjoints}
\author/literal{Kepler}
\date{2026-10-16}

\p{Let #{V,E,s,h} be a
\vocabk{finite incidence presentation}{fdip-0002}, with positive edge
weights #{w(e)>0}. Its \vocabk{incidence map}{fdip-0002} is
#{D:\mathbb R^V\to\mathbb R^E}, with matrix #{B}; put
#{W=\operatorname{diag}(w(e))}. Which adjoint returns an edge field to a
vertex field depends on the chosen pairings. The convention
#{L=B^TWB} is that of
\citet{Section 2.1, p. 4}{spielman2009effective}.}

\p{Use the \vocabk{counting inner products}{fdip-0002} on both spaces
first. The \newvocab{counting adjoint} #{D^*} is characterized by
#{\langle Df,a\rangle_E=\langle f,D^*a\rangle_V}. Exchanging finite sums
proves its matrix is #{B^T}:
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle Df,a\rangle_E
  &=\sum_e\sum_v B_{ev}f(v)a(e)\\
  &=\sum_v f(v)\sum_e B_{ev}a(e)\\
  &=\langle f,B^Ta\rangle_V.
\end{aligned}}
The characterizing identity determines the adjoint uniquely: the difference
of two possible outputs is orthogonal to every vertex field, hence to
itself, and is zero.}

\p{Alternatively, give the edge space the
\newvocab{weighted inner product}
##{\langle a,b\rangle_{E,w}=\sum_e w(e)a(e)b(e).}
It is bilinear and symmetric. Its self-pairing is a sum of nonnegative
terms; strict positivity of every weight makes it vanish only at zero.
Keep the vertex pairing counting. Then
##{\begin{aligned}
\langle Df,a\rangle_{E,w}
 &=\langle Df,Wa\rangle_E\\
 &=\langle f,B^TWa\rangle_V.
\end{aligned}}
Thus the \newvocab{weighted adjoint} is #{D_w^\dagger=B^TW}.
The \newvocab{weighted graph Laplacian} is the vertex operator
##{L=D^*WD=D_w^\dagger D=B^TWB.}
With the counting adjoint, #{D^*D} is the unit-weight operator; the
general weighted expression is #{D^*WD}. Using #{D_w^\dagger D}
instead includes the weights through the edge pairing. For a single edge
#{0\to1} of weight #{c>0}, the two adjoints send an edge value #{a}
to #{(-a,a)} and #{(-ca,ca)}, respectively.}

\p{The Laplacian does not depend on the auxiliary edge orientations.
Reverse any selection of edges, and let #{S} be the diagonal edge matrix
whose entries are #{-1} on reversed edges and #{1} otherwise. The new
incidence matrix is #{B'=SB}. Because #{S^T=S}, #{S^2=I} and
#{SWS=W},
##{\begin{aligned}
(B')^TWB'&=B^TSWSB\\
         &=B^TWB.
\end{aligned}}
An edge field representing the same oriented data changes as #{a'=Sa};
its weighted pairing is preserved. A loop has a zero incidence row
before and after reversal. Parallel edges remain distinct coordinates.
This changes orientations, not endpoints or weights.}

\p{Mathlib's \leanref{LinearMap.toMatrix_adjoint} identifies adjoint
matrices with conjugate transposes in orthonormal bases. Over the reals
this is transpose. The coordinate edge basis is orthonormal for counting,
but not generally for the weighted pairing; the finite-sum calculation
above accounts for the extra #{W}.}
